## Spawning subagents through ccc

```
ccc run <type> [--cwd DIR] [--prompt-file F | -] [PROMPT]
```

Name only the agent *type*. `ccc` chooses harness, model, effort and account
from its own config and live usage data. Never pass `--harness` or
`--profile`, never set `CCC_*` env vars — those are human debugging controls.

Stdout = the subagent's final message, then a blank line and one trailer:

```
[ccc] session=<id> harness=<h> model=<m> effort=<e> profile=<p|->
```

All diagnostics go to stderr. Keep the session id; a follow-up to the same
subagent with context intact is:

```
ccc resume <session-id> [--prompt-file F | -] [PROMPT]
```

Exit codes: 0 success; 1 subagent ran and reported failure (message on stderr);
2 config/usage/agent-definition error, nothing spawned; 3 harness produced no
usable result; 4 the subagent hit a usage limit and no other account could
continue it.

A non-zero exit, or your shell tool stopping the command, is a cut-off
launch — see **Cut-off launch** — never a reply. Exit 1, 3 or 4 may still
print the session id in the trailer; exit 2 spawned nothing. Nothing the
subagent printed before it was cut off is a status line to route on.

Invoke `ccc` as a plain command: `ccc run …` / `ccc resume …` and nothing
else. No `nohup`, no trailing `&`, no redirects, no wrapper scripts. Read
its stdout directly.

A subagent may run for an hour or more. Every `ccc run` and `ccc resume`
goes through your shell tool's long-running mode with the limit at the
tool's ceiling, and never under one hour in any case. A limit is a maximum,
not a wait; a subagent that overruns it is killed with everything beneath it
and must be resumed.
{%- if harness == "claude" %} Use `run_in_background: true` and `timeout` at
the ceiling the tool description states (`7200000` ms unless it says more);
the foreground tool's ten minutes is never enough.
{%- else %} Set the shell tool's own timeout parameter at its maximum.
{%- endif %} This matters most where the subagent
itself launches subagents — a whole orchestrator cycle runs for hours. Then
wait for it to finish and read its stdout directly — never `&`, `nohup`, a
wrapper script or a timer of your own.

The prompt is the entire contract: the subagent sees only your prompt and its
own agent definition. State the task, inputs by absolute path, what to
produce, and the absolute output path. It cannot ask you anything mid-run.

## Cut-off launch

1. **What it is.** Two kinds, told apart by who stopped the command:
   - a **failure** — `ccc` itself exited 1, 3 or 4 (a crash, a usage limit,
     a reported failed turn), or your shell tool stopped the command for any
     reason other than its time limit;
   - a **ceiling hit** — your shell tool reported, in whatever words it
     uses, that it stopped the command because it reached its time limit;
     no `ccc` exit code comes with that report. The subagent was still
     working; it, and everything it had launched, were killed with `ccc`.

   In both, the subagent's work may be intact — its files, its commits, its
   context — and `ccc resume` continues it. Two things are not a cut-off:
   exit 2 (nothing was spawned; go straight to your role's failure route),
   and a launch you ended yourself with `ccc kill` on the user's direction
   (it exits 3 too, but the user is already in the loop: **When a subagent
   hangs** applies — report and wait). A launch that has no result in your
   context when you are resumed with `Continue.` was killed together with
   you; treat it as a ceiling hit.
2. **Find the session.** The trailer, if one was printed (a ceiling hit
   prints none). Otherwise `ccc status`: every line carries the
   invocation's agent type, its `started` time, its state and `session=`.
   Yours is the invocation of the type you launched whose `started` is after
   you issued this launch; if the newest of that type is `done`, it is an
   earlier launch of yours, not this one. No such invocation, or one with
   `session=-` → there is no session; **repeat** the launch verbatim instead
   — same prompt, same target paths.
3. **Still alive?** State `running`, `stopping`, `orphaned` or `unknown`
   means the subagent is still working without you. `ccc resume` refuses
   such a session and you never kill it; you do not poll for it either.
   Report its state and session id through your role's failure route and
   stop; the user decides.
4. **Resume.** `ccc resume <session-id> "Continue."`, launched exactly as a
   run (long-running mode, limit at the ceiling). Route its reply as the
   original launch's: same step, same target paths, same routing. A resumed
   subagent that had already finished replies with its status line again.
5. **Cap.** Two counts per launch, resumes and repeats together. At most
   **two** failures per launch: the third failure of the same launch is
   final. At most **four** ceiling hits per launch: the fifth is final. A
   ceiling hit is not a failure and charges only its own count — nothing
   failed, the subagent was making progress — but a launch that is cut at
   the ceiling again and again is one whose in-flight operation never ends,
   and the user, not the next resume, decides what to do with it. Final
   means your role's failure route, with what the last attempt printed and
   the session id. A recovery that comes back with a reply ends the launch;
   neither count ever resets to allow another attempt.

An **unrecovered launch** is one that ends without a reply: exit 2, a
session still alive (step 3), or a spent cap — either count — (step 5). Your role's failure
route takes it, carrying what `ccc` last printed to stderr, verbatim, the
session id if one is known and, if the session is still alive, its state.

## When a subagent hangs

`ccc kill` exists for one situation: a subagent that has stopped making
progress and will never return — typically because a test or command it ran
is hanging. It is not part of normal operation. `ccc status` is read
whenever you need it — to recover a cut-off launch, or to find the
invocation the user means. Slow is
not hung; a large `idle` value is not evidence; you never kill a subagent to
speed things up, to retry, or because you are impatient. Act only when the
user tells you a subagent is hung and directs you to stop it. Absent that
direction, wait.

- `ccc status` lists your own subagents, running and finished — never
  anything outside your subtree, never yourself. Each line carries the
  8-character invocation id, the agent type, its state (`running`,
  `stopping`, `orphaned`, `lost`, `unknown`; `done`, `failed`, `killed`) and
  `session=<id>`. Use it to find the invocation the user means; if you
  cannot tell which one that is, ask. Do not pass `--trace`.
- `ccc kill <inv>` with the invocation id and no flags. It signals the
  subagent's whole process tree, including the hung command, waits for it
  to die, and prints `<inv> killed session=<id>`. The `ccc run` that
  launched it, if it is still waiting, then returns exit 3 with its trailer;
  that launch produced no result, and that is not a cut-off launch; do not
  resume it on your own. Report the kill and the session id to the
  user and wait for direction — resume it, relaunch fresh, or stop — rather
  than choosing yourself.
- `ccc kill --now <inv>` only if the plain kill printed `survived`, and
  only once. Go by the printed state word, not the exit code: `refused` also
  exits 1 and means ccc will not touch the process — report it, do not
  escalate. Exit 2 with no line means nothing was signalled — the id is
  wrong or outside your subtree; recheck `ccc status`, otherwise report it.
  Never `--all`, `--grace`, `--no-wait`.

**Never `pkill`, `killall`, `kill <pid>`, or any signal to a process you
found with `ps`.** Every agent on this machine — other sessions' subagents,
your own parent, and you — is the same `claude` or `codex` binary. A
name-based or guessed-pid kill is as likely to take out another session's
agent or yourself as the target. `ccc kill` identity-checks the process and
is confined to your subtree; it is the only way you stop a subagent.
